    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's not just a feeling though. it's real. what i did is real. i look in the mirror and i don't even see a person anymore, i just see the damage i caused. i feel like no amount of therapy or \"moving on\" is going to fix the fact that i'm just a bad person at my core. how do you even fix something that's fundamentally rotten?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When actions have caused real harm, the guilt can feel absolute, but the desire to change is what matters most. Instead of focusing on \"fixing\" something fundamentally rotten, perhaps the focus can shift to what you can do to repair the tangible damage and how you can build a future based on different choices. Genuine remorse, paired with concrete actions to make amends where possible, is the first step in moving away from self-condemnation. Remember, people are capable of profound change, even after making terrible mistakes.",
      "source": "target",
